      <section class="hero" id="top">
        <div class="shell hero-grid">
          <div class="hero-copy">
            <h1>Visual FoxPro&nbsp;9 decompiler.</h1>
            <p class="hero-summary">
              Compiled VFP9 binaries in. Functionally equivalent, rebuildable projects out.
            </p>
            <p class="hero-intro">
              You have a Visual FoxPro 9 application and no longer have its source. FoxLift
              reads the compiled binary — an <code>.exe</code>, <code>.app</code>,
              <code>.fxp</code>, <code>.scx</code> or <code>.vcx</code> — and gives you back
              canonical VFP source with its symbol table, laid out as a project you can open,
              read and rebuild. It runs offline, and it never guesses: anything it cannot
              recover is marked in place, naming the exact reason.
            </p>
            <div class="hero-actions">
              <a class="button" href="#start">Start here</a>
              <a class="button button-quiet" href="https://github.com/foxlift/foxlift">Open source on GitHub</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <dl class="hero-facts" aria-label="FoxLift facts">
            <div>
              <dt>10,514 / 10,514</dt>
              <dd>sections lifted, development corpus</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>99.3%</dt>
              <dd>sections lifted, held-out repos</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>No VFP needed</dt>
              <dd>to decompile — install and run</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section process" id="start">
        <div class="shell process-grid">
          <div class="process-heading" data-reveal>
            <h2>Start here.</h2>
            <p>Three commands, and no Visual FoxPro installation. FoxLift is a Python package; decompiling is entirely offline. A real VFP9 is only needed if you want to verify a result by recompiling it yourself.</p>
          </div>

          <ol class="process-list" data-reveal>
            <li>
              <span>01</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Install</h3>
                <p>Python 3.10 or newer.</p>
                <pre class="code-panel"><code>pip install foxlift</code></pre>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>02</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Look inside first</h3>
                <p>Tells you what the binary actually is and what it contains, before you commit to anything. A file that is not VFP is refused cleanly.</p>
                <pre class="code-panel"><code>foxlift inspect erp_client.exe</code></pre>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>03</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Decompile, or extract the whole project</h3>
                <p><code>decompile</code> lifts the code to a source tree. <code>extract</code> unpacks an <code>.exe</code> or <code>.app</code> into a named project tree — lifted members plus tables and resources byte-for-byte — and reassembles a <code>.pjx</code> that VFP9 rebuilds.</p>
                <pre class="code-panel"><code>foxlift decompile erp_client.exe -o recovered/
foxlift extract    erp_client.exe -o project/</code></pre>
              </div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>04</span>
              <div>
                <h3>Read the verdict</h3>
                <p>Add <code>--json</code> to any command for machine-readable output. Exit <code>0</code> with <code>"verified": true</code> means everything lifted. Exit <code>2</code> is a partial — honestly marked, never a silent failure.</p>
                <pre class="code-panel"><code>foxlift decompile erp_client.exe -o recovered/ --json
&#123;
  "outdir": "recovered/",
  "modules": 13,
  "verified": false          # exit 2 — a partial, honestly marked
&#125;</code></pre>
              </div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section story" id="output">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>What you get.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              One directory per module, each holding the recovered source, the symbol table, and a per-section verdict you can audit.
            </p>

            <pre class="code-panel"><code>recovered/
├── module_000e00/
│   ├── source.prg      # canonical VFP source
│   ├── symbols.txt     # recovered symbol table
│   └── meta.json       # per-section verdicts, with reasons
└── module_005e00/ …</code></pre>

            <p>And inside a <code>source.prg</code>:</p>

            <pre class="code-panel"><code>* --- section 1 ---
LPARAMETERS TCORDERNO
SELECT ORDERS
LOCATE FOR ORDER_NO = TCORDERNO
THIS.PARENT.TXTCUSTOMER.VALUE = ORDERS.CUST_NAME
THIS.PARENT.CDSAVE.ENABLED = .T.
MESSAGEBOX("Loaded " + TCORDERNO, 64, "Orders")
* --- section 2 (1 statement(s) beyond the supported slice: SET variant outside forced subset) ---</code></pre>

            <p>
              Abridged, but true to shape: every liftable statement comes back as canonical VFP, and anything beyond the measured slice stays in the output as an annotated placeholder naming its exact blocker. Nothing is silently dropped.
            </p>
            <p>
              Concretely: a real production <code>.exe</code> from the development corpus comes back as 13 modules and roughly 10,900 lines of source — 159 of 160 sections lifted, and the one miss labeled in place with the unmeasured SET variant that blocks it.
            </p>
            <p class="note">
              What provably cannot come back: comments are never compiled, DECLARE parameter names are discarded, WAIT WINDOW clause order is canonicalised by the compiler, and some identifiers are blanked by the compiler itself. FoxLift proves what is unrecoverable rather than inventing it.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section process" id="process">
        <div class="shell process-grid">
          <div class="process-heading" data-reveal>
            <h2>What it does.</h2>
            <p>FoxLift follows a measured path from binary containers back to buildable VFP projects. The standard is simple: recovered projects must rebuild and behave like the compiled input — readable guesses are not enough.</p>
          </div>

          <ol class="process-list" data-reveal>
            <li>
              <span>01</span>
              <div><h3>Read</h3><p>Find modules, forms, classes, reports, menus, and resources.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>02</span>
              <div><h3>Lift</h3><p>Decode measured bytecode, symbols, expressions, and control flow.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>03</span>
              <div><h3>Write</h3><p>Emit functionally equivalent VFP source and project artifacts.</p></div>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span>04</span>
              <div><h3>Verify</h3><p>Rebuild in VFP9 and compare canonical bytecode.</p></div>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section story" id="receipts">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>Round-trip, with receipts.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              The repo ships a small authored demo project. Compile it on VFP9, decompile it with the CLI, recompile the output: statement frames and symbol tables come back byte-identical. Exit codes, hashes, and per-section verdicts are committed alongside it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Every benchmark corpus is public VFP code on GitHub. On the development corpus — <a href="https://github.com/luhongbin/YiFeiERP">luhongbin/YiFeiERP</a>, one vendor's real ERP estate of forms and class libraries — 10,514 of 10,514 method sections lift, none blocked, none silently dropped. On repositories never used to infer schemas — <a href="https://github.com/VFPX/AATest">VFPX/AATest</a>, <a href="https://github.com/RubenAlejandroFalcon/ARoMal">ARoMal</a>, and <a href="https://github.com/VFPX/ReportingApps">VFPX/ReportingApps</a> for validation, plus held-out repos such as <a href="https://github.com/ankuryu/vfpObjbill">vfpObjbill</a> opened once per round and never tuned on — every validation section lifts (2,007 of 2,007) and 1,023 of 1,030 held-out sections lift (99.3%). Recompiling the decompiled output on a real VFP9 reproduces the original bytecode frames for 29,595 of 29,597 development form and class sections; every remaining difference is a documented, oracle-proven loss in compilation, never a guess.
            </p>
            <p>
              Some things provably cannot come back: comments are never compiled, DECLARE parameter names are discarded, WAIT WINDOW clause order is canonicalised by the compiler, and some identifiers are blanked by the compiler itself. FoxLift proves what is unrecoverable and never invents it.
            </p>
            <a class="button" href="https://github.com/foxlift/foxlift/tree/main/demo">See the demo receipts</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section story" id="status">
        <div class="shell story-grid">
          <div class="story-copy" data-reveal>
            <h2>Where we stand.</h2>
            <p class="lead">
              Method code comes back today and whole applications extract to a rebuildable project tree; report definitions are the remaining gap. In between, partial results are the contract, not a failure mode.
            </p>

            <ol class="timeline" aria-label="Project status timeline">
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Aug 27</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.1.0 — first public release</h3>
                  <p>
                    Forms and class libraries (.scx/.vcx), compiled programs (.fxp), and module
                    scans of .app/.exe decompile to per-module VFP source with symbols and
                    per-section verdicts. Round-trip demo with committed
                    receipts. On <a href="https://pypi.org/project/foxlift/">PyPI</a> and <a href="https://github.com/foxlift/foxlift">GitHub</a>.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Aug 28</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.2.0 — compiled programs and menus</h3>
                  <p>
                    DEFINE CLASS scaffolds in .fxp, compiled menu programs (.mpx), SQL decode, and
                    non-Latin code pages. Recompiling the decompiled output on real VFP9
                    reproduces the original bytecode frames for 95% of the measured population.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Aug 31</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.3.0 — whole applications, and closed parity</h3>
                  <p>
                    <code>foxlift extract</code> unpacks an .exe or .app into a named project
                    tree — compiled members lifted back to source, tables and resources
                    byte-for-byte — and the library reassembles a .pjx that VFP9 rebuilds
                    headless. Parity on the development corpus is closed: every method section
                    lifts, 29,579 of 29,597 form and class sections and 2,007 of 2,007
                    validation sections recompile frame-equal, and every remaining difference
                    is a documented, oracle-proven loss in compilation.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Sep 4</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.4.0 — a second corpus, and conservation</h3>
                  <p>
                    A second benchmark corpus — 29 public repositories, 170,820 modules —
                    measured under the same rules: 101,719 of 103,363 development
                    sections lift with zero leaked bytes. Every declared member of every
                    module on both corpora now leaves a trace in the lifted source. The
                    held-out repositories, opened once at the wave close, lift 1,015 of
                    1,030 sections. Dozens of statement and expression banks measured whole
                    on the oracle: the SET family, BROWSE and popup menus, the DO family,
                    REPORT FORM, SQL unions and join chains, the scope operator, and the
                    folded LINENO() line layout.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Sep 6</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.5.0 — the wave-2 close, measured on both corpora</h3>
                  <p>
                    Every law of the second wave measured together on one tree: 102,798 of
                    103,363 development sections of the second corpus lift with zero leaked
                    bytes, and blocked sections fell from 1,644 to 565. Both sealed splits
                    opened once, with no tuning after: the held-out repositories lift 1,022
                    of 1,030 sections, the sealed second-corpus split 13,809 of 13,978.
                    Member conservation holds on both corpora and on the sealed split. New
                    readings: the SQL SELECT column and destination forms, CREATE CURSOR
                    field banks from one declarative table, the remaining statement leads,
                    the assignment-target spellings, 159 measured function arities, and
                    constant folds written back from an oracle-confirmed table of preimages.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Sep 12</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>v0.6.0 — the wave-3 close</h3>
                  <p>
                    Five decoder lanes measured together on one tree: 103,015 of 103,363
                    development sections of the second corpus lift with zero leaked bytes, and
                    blocked sections fell from 565 to 348 across 92 refusal classes. Both sealed
                    splits opened once with no tuning after — the held-out repositories lift
                    1,023 of 1,030, the sealed second-corpus split 13,845 of 13,978, where three
                    refusal classes emptied and none grew. New readings: the expression reader's
                    value and statement sides, the CAST channel and the round-78 residue, CREATE
                    CURSOR field tails, and the work queue's five largest families — DO WITH,
                    the SET ids, UPDATE SET, USE tails and DO CASE.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li data-now>
                <p class="timeline-date">Now</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>Where things stand</h3>
                  <p>
                    Wave 3 is closed. On the second corpus the lift stands at 99.66% and the
                    blocked sections at 348 in 92 classes over 257 records — down from 7,867 at
                    the first wave's close. On the first corpus every development and validation
                    section lifts, and the table path stands at 29,595 of 29,597. The residual is
                    still mostly one class deep: closing a single refusal class would fully
                    unblock 211 of the 257 remaining records. Wave 4 takes the work queue's top
                    three — the unterminated 43 group, RECALL trailing bytes and unresolved
                    INSERT column lists — then hardens the table-path instrument and reads the
                    frame-shape families the tables keep. Each family is measured whole against
                    a real VFP9 oracle before it ships, and every figure on this page comes from
                    that measurement.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p class="timeline-date">Next</p>
                <div class="timeline-body">
                  <h3>Reports and the rest</h3>
                  <p>
                    Report definitions (.frx) and the remaining artifact types close out the
                    set, so a whole application round-trips without exceptions.
                  </p>
                </div>
              </li>
            </ol>

            <p>
              Hand FoxLift any binary and it tells the truth about it. A file that is not VFP is refused cleanly. A VFP binary always yields whatever is provable: every module found is decompiled, and any statement beyond the measured slice stays in the output as an annotated placeholder naming the exact blocker — never silently dropped. Exit 0 with a verified verdict means a 100% lift; exit 2 marks a partial.
            </p>
            <p>
              Concretely: a real production .exe from the development corpus currently comes back as 13 modules and roughly 10,900 lines of source — 159 of 160 sections lifted, and the one miss is labeled in place with the unmeasured SET variant that blocks it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Scope is Visual FoxPro 9 (SP2). Every claim above is measured against a real VFP9 compiler; nothing is guessed.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

